Here is an example of an embedded C program that turns a lamp connected to a relay at port pin P1.0 on and off with the highest delay generated using Timer 0 in mode 0:
```
#include <reg51.h>

sbit lamp = P1^0; // output pin connected to the relay and lamp

void delay(unsigned int time) // function to generate delay
{
    unsigned int i;
    for(i=0; i<time; i++)
    {
        TMOD = 0x00; // Timer 0 in mode 0
        TH0 = 0xFF; // set the highest value for delay
        TL0 = 0xFF;
        TR0 = 1; // start Timer 0
        while(TF0 == 0); // wait for the timer to overflow
        TR0 = 0; // stop Timer 0
        TF0 = 0; // clear the overflow flag
    }
}

void main()
{
    while(1) // infinite loop
    {
        lamp = 1; // turn on the lamp
        delay(1); // delay for the highest value
        lamp = 0; // turn off the lamp
        delay(1); // delay for the highest value
    }
}
```
In this program, an output pin is defined to control the relay and the lamp. The lamp is turned on and off in an infinite loop with the highest delay generated using Timer 0 in mode 0 between each change.

In the main function, an infinite loop is used to toggle the output pin that is connected to the relay, causing the lamp to turn on and off with the highest delay.

The `delay()` function uses Timer 0 in mode 0 and sets the highest value for the delay by writing 0xFF to the TH0 and TL0 registers. The timer is started and the program waits for the timer to overflow using the TF0 flag. The timer is then stopped and the overflow flag is cleared. The delay passed to the delay function is the number of times this process is repeated.

